[[Image:Jaguar logo.JPG|250px|]]&lt;br&gt;
The '''SEPECAT Jaguar''' is an Anglo-French ground attack aircraft in service with the [[Royal Air Force]] and several export customers, notably the [[Indian Air Force|Indian]] and [[Royal Air Force of Oman|Omani Air Force]]. It was the product of the world's first bi-national military aircraft program. France retired the Jaguar on [[July 1]] [[2005]].

==Background==
The Jaguar program began in the early [[1960s]], in response to a British requirement for an advanced supersonic jet trainer, and a French need for a cheap, subsonic dual role trainer and attack aircraft with good short field performance. From these apparently disparate aims would come a single and entirely different aircraft: relatively high-tech, supersonic, and optimised for ground attack in a high-threat environment. It was planned as a replacement for the RAF [[Hawker Hunter]] and the Armee de l'Air [[F-100 Super Sabre]].

Cross-channel negotiations led to the formation of '''SEPECAT''' (the ''Société Européenne de Production de l'Avion d'Ecole de Combat et d'Appui Tactique'') in [[1966 in aviation|1966]] as a joint venture between [[Breguet|Bréguet]] (the design leader) and the [[British Aircraft Corporation]] to produce the airframe, and a separate teaming of [[Rolls-Royce plc|Rolls-Royce]] and [[Turbomeca|Turboméca]] to develop the [[Rolls-Royce/Turbomeca Adour|Adour]] afterburning turbofan engine.

The first of 8 prototypes flew on [[September 8]] [[1968 in aviation|1968]]. It was an orthodox single-seat, swept-wing, twin-engine design but with tall landing gear. It had a maximum take-off weight in the 15 tonne class and could manage a combat radius on internal fuel alone of 850 km. Maximum speed was Mach 1.6 (Mach 1.1 at sea level) and hardpoints were fitted for an external weapons load of up to 10 lbs.

==Into service==
The Armee de l'Air took delivery of the first production Jaguar in [[1973 in aviation|1973]], one of an eventual 160 single-seat '''Jaguar A'''s. For type conversion training, France also took 40 of the two-seat '''Jaguar E'''. The RAF accepted delivery of the first of 165 single-seat '''Jaguar GR.1'''s (or '''Jaguar S''') with 54(F) squadron in [[1974]]. These were supplemented by 35 two-seat trainers, the '''Jaguar T2''' (or '''Jaguar B''' according to the manufacturer's designation). In December 1983 75 RAF Jaguars were updated to the '''GR.1A''' and  '''T.1A''' standard.  The upgraded GR.3/A recently entred service for the RAF, the upgrade included new engines, [[GPS]], Terrain Referenced Navigation [[(TRN)]], and [[Night vision goggles]] compatibility. After Breguet was purchased by [[Dassault Aviation|Dassault]], the proposed '''Jaguar M''' variant, a carrier version for the French [[Aeronavale]], was cancelled in favor of the [[Dassault Super Étendard]].

Jaguars were also sold with some success, to a number of overseas countries. The largest single customer was India, which built the Jaguar under license in India. Around about 100 Jaguars were built by [[Hindustan Aeronautics|Hindustan Aeronautics Limited]] as the '''Shamsher'''. The '''Jaguar International''' was an export version which was sold to Ecuador, India, Nigeria and Oman.

The Jaguar saw combat during the [[Gulf War]], with the Armée de l'Air and RAF and the [[Kosovo War]] with the Armée de l'Air.

==Replacement==
[[Image:Jaguargrounddisplay.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Jaguar as part of a RAF touring display]] 
The aircraft has been updated several times and remains in front-line service with Britain. It is to be replaced by the [[Eurofighter Typhoon]] in the RAF and the [[Dassault Rafale|Rafale]] in the Armée de l'Air.

Demands by the UK [[HM Treasury|Treasury]] demanding cuts in the defence budget led to reports that the Jaguar was a possible candidate for early retirement. Announcing plans for the future of the British military on [[July 21]] [[2004 in aviation|2004]] Defence Secretary [[Geoff Hoon]] detailed plans to withdraw the Jaguar by [[2007]].

Critics say the aircraft is near the end of its service life and does not have all the capabilites required of a front line jet. Proponents argue that the aircraft has been recently updated and is the most cost effective of all the RAF's fast jet force.

==Units using the Jaguar==
{{Commons|SEPECAT Jaguar}}
===Royal Air Force===


* [[No._6_Squadron_RAF|RAF No. 6 Squadron]]
* [[No._41_Squadron_RAF|RAF No. 41 Squadron]] (due to reform as the Fast Jet and Weapons OEU in March 2006)

===[[Indian Airforce]]===

==Operators==
* France, India, Nigeria, Oman, United Kingdom and Ecuador

