      <contributor>
        <username>Copsewood</username>
        <id>7716</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>revert to previous version by The Tom see discussion</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Michael Linton''' is the designer of a [[Local Exchange Trading Systems|Local Exchange Trading System]] (LETS) known as [[LETSystem]], an open form of money, or personal and practical arrangement of community currency. The first of very many instances of this design was originated in [[Comox Valley]], BC, Canada in 1983. 

Many if not most of the hundreds of LETS within the UK, Canada and Australia and probably thousands of similar systems elsewhere, involve a members' agreement identical to or based closely on Michael Linton's original LETSystem design.


==External links==
*[http://www.gmlets.u-net.com/ LETSystem]
*[http://www.openmoney.org Open money systems]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Claude Servais Mathias Pouillet</title>
    <id>176236</id>
    <revision>
      <id>33966461</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-05T12:10:44Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>YurikBot</username>
        <id>271058</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>robot  Adding: nl</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[image:Claude_Servais_Mathias_Pouillet.jpg|thumb|200px|Claude Servais Mathias Pouillet.]]
'''Claude Servais Mathias Pouillet''' ([[February 16]], [[1791]]&amp;ndash;[[June 14]], [[1868]]) was a [[France|French]] [[physicist]].

Pouillet was born in [[Cusance]], [[Doubs]], [[France]].

Between [[1837]] and [[1838]] he made, independently of [[John Herschel|John Frederick William Herschel]] ([[1792]]-[[1871]]), the first quantitative measurements of the [[heat]] emitted by the [[Sun]]. His value was circa half the actual one because the level of the [[Earth's atmosphere|atmospheric]] [[absorption]] was uncertain those times, and the problem wasn't solved until [[1888]] and [[1904]]. With the [[Dulong-Petit law]] he wrongly estimated the [[temperature]] of the Sun's surface to be around 1800 [[Celsius|&amp;deg;C]]. This value was corrected in [[1879]] to 5430 &amp;deg;C by [[Joseph Stefan|Jo&amp;#382;ef Stefan]] ([[1835]]-[[1893]]).

Poillet works include ''On atmospheric electricity'', (London [[1832]]), ''Éléments de physique expérimentale et de météorologie'', (Paris [[1856]]). 

Pouillet died in [[Paris]], France.

See also: [[pyrheliometer]], [[Stefan-Boltzmann law]], [[galvanometer|tangent galvanometer]].

[[Category:1791 births|Pouillet, Claude Servais Mathias]]
[[Category:1868 deaths|Pouillet, Claude Servais Mathias]]

[[de:Claude Servais Mathias Pouillet]]
[[fr:Claude Pouillet]]
[[nl:Claude Pouillet]]
[[sl:Claude Servais Mathias Pouillet]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Neal Boortz</title>
    <id>176237</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41822549</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-01T23:44:13Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Dl2000</username>
        <id>917223</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>subcat</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Neal Boortz''' (born [[April 6]], [[1945]]), is a popular and controversial [[United States|American]] [[talk radio]] host based in [[Atlanta, Georgia]]. His self-given [[nickname]]s include: The Talkmaster, Mighty Whitey, The Mouth of the South, America's Rude Awakening and the High Priest of the Church of the Painful Truth.

== Life ==
Boortz was born in [[Bryn Mawr, Pennsylvania]], but, since his was a military family, he was raised elsewhere, in many locations throughout the country. He was a self-described &quot;marine brat.&quot; In his pre-radio life, he was a speech writer for [[Governor of Georgia|Georgia Governor]] [[Lester Maddox]].
He began his radio career in [[College Station, Texas]] in the 1960s at WTAW-AM.  Boortz also practiced law from [[1977]] to [[1992]].
His syndicated show originates from [[WSB-AM]] 750 in Atlanta, and currently has about five million listeners nationwide. Boortz also occasionally writes a column on the Internet news/commentary site ''[[Townhall.com]]''.

Boortz has a wife named Donna (&quot;She Who Must Be Obeyed&quot;, as he calls her on his program), with whom he has one daughter, Laura. Boortz is an avid [[golf]]er and [[aviation]] enthusiast.
He is an accomplished [[Aviator|pilot]] with over 3,000 hours flying as pilot-in-command, and donates time (both as a pilot and fund-raiser) in support of [[Angel Flight]].  He holds a commercial pilot certificate in single-engine airplanes, with an instrument rating, and is also certificated in lighter-than-air free balloons. Boortz is an ardent supporter of [[DeKalb Peachtree Airport]] in Atlanta, Georgia.

== Politics ==
A self-described [[Libertarian]], Boortz politics tends to side with [[liberals]] and [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] on social issues such as [[abortion]] (about which Boortz will not allow phone calls), [[freedom of speech]], and the [[Separation of church and state]]. In line with the traditional views of the [[United States Libertarian Party]], Boortz supports an end to the [[war on drugs]] and, along with the views of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], supports lower [[taxes]], smaller [[government]], emphasis on &quot;individual responsibility&quot;, and the [[rule of law]].

Boortz is often accused of being a &quot;Republican in sheep's clothing.&quot; He has recently been described using the term [[neolibertarian]]&lt;!--analagous to &quot;neoconservative&quot;??--&gt;.  This refers to his tendency to advocate Republican party platforms and to his frequent (but not unilateral) support of Republican politicians, especially [[George W. Bush]].  Boortz's post-[[September 11, 2001 attacks|9/11]] politics include support for the US-led [[Gulf War II|War in Iraq]] and a more aggressive foreign policy. These views put him in conflict with the Libertarian Party. For instance, [[Justin Raimondo]] of [[Antiwar.com]] has called Boortz a &quot;statist, not a libertarian&quot; and a &quot;liberventionist&quot; and has urged the Libertarian Party to &quot;Boot Boortz&quot;[http://www.antiwar.com/justin/jspecial112603.html].  Boortz counters that the issues of the greatest importance today, after the 9/11 attacks, are those in which terrorism has dominated.

== Radio Personality ==
''The Neal Boortz Show'' features Boortz, co-producers [[Royal Marshall]] and [[Belinda Skelton]], interviewees, and callers. 
On the air and on his [http://www.boortz.com website] Boortz admonishes, &quot;Don't believe anything you read on this web page, or, for that matter, anything you hear on The Neal Boortz Show, unless it is consistent with what you already know to be true, or unless you have taken the time to research the matter to prove its accuracy to your own satisfaction.&quot;  On numerous occasions Boortz has cautioned his listeners to take no heed nor place any credence in anything he says, as he is merely an &quot;entertainer&quot;. 

Boortz can frequently be heard insulting &quot;[[public school|government schools]]&quot;, liberals, smokers, [[social welfare provision|welfare]] recipients, and others. He also creates controversy among his many listeners in [[U.S._Southern_states|the South]] for his staunch support of [[gay rights]] and for his negative comments regarding Southerners, [[Baptists]], and [[Confederate]] issues (such as governmental support of the [[Confederate flag]]).

== Author ==
Boortz is the author of a book entitled ''The Terrible Truth About Liberals''. His latest book (co-authored by Georgia Congressman [[John Linder]]) entitled ''The [[FairTax]] Book'', explains the proposal to implement a national retail sales tax in lieu of the federal income taxes, payroll taxes, estate tax, etc.  That book spent at least two weeks atop the ''[[New York Times]]'' bestseller list.

==Controversial statements==

Several days prior to Boortz's [[October 13]], [[2005]] broadcast, the [[Department of Homeland Security]] determined there was a threat to the [[New York City Subway|New York subway system]]. The general public was not officially notified for several days about this threat. However, as described by the ''[[New York Daily News]]'', &quot;a select crowd of business and arts executives&quot; were notified early on about the threat.&lt;!-- gee, I hope by reworking the PP I didn't kill the facts--&gt; On the air, Boortz declared, &quot;This is as it should be.&quot;  He went on to describe poor people in the United States as &quot;a drag on society&quot; and people who &quot;sit around all the time waiting for somebody else to take care of them.&quot;  His reasoning behind the comments was that in the event of a cataclysmic catastrophe, the rich would be needed to rebuild society and provide jobs [http://mediamatters.org/items/200510140006].

He alleges that [[Attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder|ADD and ADHD]] are &quot;medical fraud.&quot; &lt;!-- any other non-political controversial statements?? --&gt; Homeless persons are referred to as &quot;urban outdoorsmen&quot; and public education as &quot;tax payer funded child abuse&quot;. Other ''Boortzisms'' can be found [http://boortz.com/more/boortztionary.html here].  [[Media Matters for America]] maintains a list of other controversial statements by Boortz&lt;!-- complete with annotated criticism--&gt; [http://mediamatters.org/issues_topics/search_results?qstring=neal+boortz here].

== External links ==
*[http://boortz.com/ Neal Boortz website]
*[http://www.wsbradio.com/ 750 WSB Radio] - Boortz's &quot;home&quot; station
*[http://www.sourcewatch.org/index.php?title=Neal_Boortz &quot;Neal Boortz&quot;] - article at [[SourceWatch]]

