  <page>
    <title>Barossa Valley</title>
    <id>176243</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41085306</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-24T23:27:27Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Scharks</username>
        <id>727691</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>links</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Tanunda.jpg|thumb|400px|Tanunda is surrounded by vineyards showing Autumn colour.]][[Image:Bethany, Barossa Valley.jpg|thumb|400px|Another view over the valley from [[Mengler's Hill]]]]
The '''Barossa Valley''' is a major [[list of wine-producing regions|wine-producing region]] and [[tourism|tourist]] destination of [[South Australia]], located 60 km northeast of [[Adelaide]].

==History==
The Barossa Valley takes its name from the [[Barossa Ranges]], which were named by [[William Light|Colonel William Light]] in 1837. Light chose the name in memory of the British victory over the French in the ''[[Battle of Barrosa|Battle of ''Barrosa'']]'', in which he fought in 1811.

The three major towns of the Barossa each have a distinctive personality. [[Tanunda, South Australia|Tanunda]] is generally recognised as the most [[German people|German]] of the three with long-standing traditions dating back to the [[1840s]] when the first German settlers arrived in the area. The German influence survives to this day (''see [[Barossa Deutsch]]''). [[Angaston, South Australia|Angaston]], in contrast, is considered the [[England|English]] town as it was settled predominantly by [[Cornish]] miners and others from [[Britain]]. The third town, [[Nuriootpa, South Australia|Nuriootpa]], was influenced by both the German and British settlers, and today is the commercial hub of the Barossa where most of the larger stores are located. 

==Wine industry==
The [[wine]] industry plays a major role in the Barossa, being the main source of employment for many residents.  The many hectares of [[vineyard]] are the most distinctive feature of the area, especially when viewed from the [[Mengler's Hill]] lookout positioned on the range of hills that form one side of the valley itself.   The success of the wine industry has historically been celebrated every two years (odd numbers) with a week-long [[Vintage Festival]].  The festival draws visitors from all over the world and has entertainment for all tastes including a huge street parade, concerts and gourmet dining.  

===Wineries===
Among the well known [[winery|wineries]] located in the Barossa valley are Grant Burge, [[Orlando Wines|Orlando]] (producers of [[Jacob's Creek (wine)|Jacob's Creek]]), Kaesler, Penfolds, Peter Lehmann, [[Wolf Blass]], Rockford, Elderton, [[Yalumba]] and Seppelts. 

==See also==
*[[Australian wine]]

== External links ==
*[http://www.sa.gov.au South Australia Central]
*[http://www.southaustralia.com South Australian Tourism Commission]
*[http://barosr.ilisys.com.au Barossa Wine &amp; Tourism Association]
