  <page>
    <title>Normal lens</title>
    <id>176351</id>
    <revision>
      <id>26519257</id>
      <timestamp>2005-10-26T10:49:27Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>FlaBot</username>
        <id>228773</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>robot  Adding: eo</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In  [[photography]] and [[cinematography]] a '''normal lens''' is a [[photographic lens|lens]] that generates images that are generally held to have a &quot;natural&quot; perspective compared with lenses with longer or shorter focal lengths.

Lenses of shorter focal length are called ''[[wide-angle lens]]es'', while longer focal length lenses are called ''[[telephoto lens]]es''.

In still [[photography]], a '''normal lens''' is a [[photographic lens|lens]] whose [[focal length]] is roughly equivalent to the diagonal of the image projected within the camera. This roughly approximates the perceived field of view of the human eye.

Standard normal lenses for various [[film format]]s for photography are:

{| cellSpacing=&quot;0&quot; cellPadding=&quot;5&quot; border=&quot;1&quot;
|-
! Film format !! Image dimensions !! Image diagonal !! Normal lens focal length
|-
| [[Advanced Photo System|APS]] C
| 1.67 cm x 2.51 cm
| 30.15 mm
| 28 mm, 35 mm
|-
| [[135 film|135]]
| 2.4 cm x 3.6 cm
| 43.27 mm
| 50 mm, 45 mm
|-
| [[120 film|120/220]], 6 x 4.5 (645)
| 5.6 cm x 4.2 cm
| 70.00 mm
| 75 mm
|-
