For a [[135 film|35 mm]] [[camera]] with a diagonal of 43 mm, the most commonly used ''normal'' lens is 50 mm, but focal lengths between about 40 and 58 mm are also considered ''normal''.

The 50 mm focal length was chosen by [[Oscar Barnack]], the creator of the [[Leica]] camera, as a compromise between the theoretical value and good [[sharpness (visual)|sharpness]], as [[Lens (optics)|lens]] technology at the time was such that slightly longer focal lengths were able to achieve optimum sharpness.

In [[cinematography]] a [[focal length]] roughly equivalent to twice the diagonal of the image projected within the [[camera]] is considered normal.

See also: [[angle of view]], [[photographic lens]].

[[Category:Photographic lenses]]

