  <page>
    <title>A Staircase in Surrey</title>
    <id>176363</id>
    <revision>
      <id>28247118</id>
      <timestamp>2005-11-13T23:32:10Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Guanabot</username>
        <id>82928</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Robot-assisted disambiguation: Tolkien</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''''A Staircase in Surrey''''' is a sequence of five [[novel]]s by 
[[Scotland|Scottish]] [[novelist]] and academic [[J. I. M. Stewart]] ([[1906]]&amp;ndash;[[1994]]), and published between [[1974]] and [[1978]]. The title refers to student accommodation in an imaginary [[University of Oxford|Oxford]] college. (A staircase, in the more traditionally designed colleges, would identify a group of students' rooms, leading off a [[quadrangle]].)

{{spoiler}}

The narrator and central character is [[playwright]] Duncan Patullo, who returns to his old college, after a long absence, for the [[gaudy]] celebrations.  On arrival, he discovers that an old acquaintance, fellow [[Scotland|Scot]] Ranald McKechnie, now a lecturer at the college, is married to Patullo's one-time girlfriend, Janet. The second novel, ''Young Patullo'', recaps on the story of their former relationship and Patullo's undergraduate career.  Celebrated fantasy writer and Oxford don [[J. R. R. Tolkien]] appears as elderly &quot;Professor Timberlake.&quot;

During his extended stay in Oxford, Patullo meets some of his former tutors as well as some current undergraduates, and becomes embroiled in the internal affairs of the college. The discovery of a lost masterpiece by  [[Piero della Francesca]] proves crucial to the college's future fortunes, and Patullo is able to help when it is stolen.

The books, in order of publication, are:

*''The Gaudy''
*''Young Patullo''
*''A Memorial Service''
*''The Madonna of the Astrolabe''
*''Full Term''


[[Category:Novel sequences|Staircase in Surrey]]
[[Category:British novels|Staircase in Surrey]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Pavement</title>
    <id>176364</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39334055</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-12T12:15:14Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>CambridgeBayWeather</username>
        <id>294180</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/84.64.37.218|84.64.37.218]] ([[User talk:84.64.37.218|talk]]) to last version by Dcoetzee</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Pavement''' may refer to:

* [[Pavement (material)]]: An American English term referring to the durable surfacing of [[road]]s and walkways.
* [[Sidewalk|Pavement (British English)]]: A British English term for a walkway along the side of a road (which Americans call [[sidewalk]]s).
* [[Pavement marker (roads)]]: Devices placed on pavement to convey information (see also [[Lane#Lane markings|Lane markings]])
* [[Pavement (architecture)]]: A [[floor]]-like indoor or outdoor stone or tile structure.
* [[Pavement (band)]]: An [[indie-rock]] band from [[Stockton]], [[California]].

{{disambig}}</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Gaudy</title>
    <id>176366</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39763468</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-15T18:39:54Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>70.146.108.81</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Oxbridge Reunion */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Gaudy''' is a term used to describe anything as showy in a tasteless or [[vulgar]] way. In old English the noun «gaude» meant ornament or trinket. In later English its spelling became «gaud» and was accompanied by the adjective «gaudy». Much more recent folk etymology wrongly attributes the word to the name and work of [[Catalonian]] [[architect]] [[Antonio Gaudi]] ([[1852]]-[[1926]]).

== Oxbridge Reunion ==
A '''gaudy''' (from the [[Latin]], &quot;gaudere&quot;, meaning &quot;to rejoice&quot;) is a kind of reunion, particularly associated with the colleges of [[University of Oxford|Oxford]] and [[University of Cambridge|Cambridge]]. The origin of the term may be connected to the traditional University graduation song, ''Gaudeamus''.
Gaudy celebrations normally take place during the summer vacation, and may include events such as speeches, lectures, concerts and dinners.
{{UK-university-stub}}
Also an extreme form of &quot;Dolly Time&quot; where you take your anger out on persons and objects.</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
