  <page>
    <title>Poiseuille's law</title>
    <id>176410</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34659631</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-10T21:09:14Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Naconkantari</username>
        <id>676502</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/12.154.100.15|12.154.100.15]] ([[User talk:12.154.100.15|talk]]) to last version by Pjedicke</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''Poiseuille's law''' (or the '''Hagen-Poiseuille law''' also named after [[Gotthilf Heinrich Ludwig Hagen]] ([[1797]]-[[1884]]) for his experiments in [[1839]]) is the [[physical law]] concerning the voluminal [[laminar]] [[stationary]] flow ''&amp;Phi;''&lt;sub&gt;V&lt;/sub&gt; of [[potential flow|incompressible]] uniform [[viscosity|viscous]] liquid (so called [[Newtonian fluid]]) through a cylindrical tube with the constant circular cross-section, experimentally derived in [[1838]], formulated and published in [[1840]] and [[1846]] by [[Jean Louis Marie Poiseuille]] ([[1797]]-[[1869]]), and defined by:

:&lt;math&gt; \Phi_{V} = {dV\over dt} = v_{s}\pi r^{2} = {\pi r^{4}\over 8 \eta} \left( - { d p^{\star} \over dz}\right) = {\pi r^{4}\over 8 \eta} { \Delta p^{\star} \over \ell} \; , &lt;/math&gt;

where ''V'' is a volume of the liquid, poured in the time unit ''t'', ''v''&lt;sub&gt;s&lt;/sub&gt; median fluid [[velocity]] along the axial [[cylindrical coordinate system|cylindrical coordinate]] ''z'', ''r'' internal radius of the tube, &amp;Delta;''p''&lt;sup&gt;*&lt;/sup&gt; the pressure drop at the two ends, &amp;eta; dynamic fluid viscosity and ''l'' characteristic length along ''z'', a linear dimension in a cross-section (in non-cylindrical tube). The law can be derived from the [[Darcy-Weisbach equation]], developed in the field of [[hydraulics]] and which is otherwise valid for all types of flow, and also expressed in the form:

:&lt;math&gt; \Lambda = {64\over {\it Re}} \; , \quad\quad Re = {2\rho v_{s} r\over \eta} \; , &lt;/math&gt;

where ''Re'' is the [[Reynolds number]] and ''&amp;rho;'' fluid density. In this form the law approximates the ''[[Darcy friction factor]]'', the ''energy (head) loss factor'', ''friction loss factor'' or ''Darcy (friction) factor'' &amp;Lambda; in the laminar flow at very low velocities in cylindrical tube. The theoretical derivation of slightly different Poiseuille's original form of the law was made independently by Wiedman in [[1856]] and Neumann and E. Hagenbach in [[1858]] ([[1859]], [[1860]]). Hagenbach was the first who called this law the Poiseuille's law.

The law is also very important specially in [[hemorheology]] and [[haemodynamics|hemodynamics]], both fields of [[physiology]].

The Poiseuilles' law was later in [[1891]] extended to [[turbulent flow]] by L. R. Wilberforce, based on Hagenbach's work. 

== Curiosity ==

The law itself shows what an interesting field this is, as the Darcy-Weisbach equation was studied by a number of other scientists, including Chézy, Weisbach, Darcy, Poiseuille, Hagen, Reynolds, Fanning, Prandtl, Blasius, von Kármán, Nikuradse, Colebrook, White, Rouse, and Moody.

Also note in the formula how strongly the flow depends on the radius. If all else is held constant, a doubling of the radius of the channel results in a ''sixteen-fold'' increase in the flow.

== Relation to [[electrical circuit|electrical circuits]] ==	 

Electricity was originally understood to be a kind of fluid. This [[hydraulic analogy]] is still of some use in teaching.

Poiseuille's law corresponds to [[Ohm's law]] for electrical circuits, where pressure drop &amp;Delta;''p''&lt;sup&gt;*&lt;/sup&gt; is somehow replaced by [[voltage]] ''V'' and voluminal flow rate &amp;Phi;&lt;sub&gt;V&lt;/sub&gt; by [[Current (electricity)|current]] ''I''. According to this a term 8&amp;eta; ''l''/&amp;pi;''r''&lt;sup&gt;4&lt;/sup&gt; is an adequate substitution for the [[electrical resistance]] ''R''.

==References==
*Sutera, Salvatore P., &quot;The history of Poiseuille's law,&quot; ''Annual Review of Fluid Mechanics'', Vol. 25, 1993, pp. 1-19


[[Category:Fluid dynamics]]
[[Category:Eponymous laws]]

[[de:Gesetz von Hagen-Poiseuille]]
[[es:Ley de Poiseuille]]
[[nl:Wet van Hagen-Poiseuille]]
[[pl:Prawo Hagena-Poiseuille'a]]
[[sl:Poiseuillov zakon]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
