  <page>
    <title>Leon Czolgosz</title>
    <id>176461</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40684686</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-22T07:38:54Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Polotet</username>
        <id>531924</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>agree with comment on talk page--assassins doesn't have that kind of established chronology</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Leonczolgosz44.JPG|frame|Photograph of Leon Czolgosz.]]
[[Image:McKinleyAssassination.jpg|thumb|right|175px|Leon Czolgosz shoots President McKinley with a concealed revolver, at the Pan-American Exposition.]]
[[Image:Leonczolgosz55.JPG|frame|Police booking photograph ([[mug shot]]) of Leon Czolgosz.]]
[[Image:First photograph of Leon F. Czolgosz, the assassin of President William McKinley, in jail.jpg|thumb|300px|First photograph of Leon F. Czolgosz in jail]]

'''Leon Frank Czołgosz''' ([[1873]]&amp;ndash;[[October 29]], [[1901]]) was the [[assassin]] of [[President of the United States|U.S. President]] [[William McKinley]]. A [[Republican Party (United States)| registered Republican]], in the last few years of his life he was heavily influenced by [[anarchist]] writers such as [[Emma Goldman]].  

One of seven children of [[Poland|Polish]] immigrants, Czołgosz (Polish, pronounced {{IPA|/ʧɔʊ̯gɔʃ/}}, often anglicized to {{IPA|/ʧoɫgɑʃ/}}) was born in [[Detroit, Michigan|Detroit]], [[Michigan]].  His actual date of birth is unknown - some sources state, without offering any evidence, that he was born on 1st January 1873.  He lived in conditions of brutal poverty most of his life.  He left his family farm in [[Cleveland, Ohio|Cleveland]], [[Ohio]] at age ten to work at American Steel and Wire Company with two of his brothers. When Czolgosz was 12, his mother died while giving birth to another child. At the height of his employment, he was making $4 a day, but after the workers of his factory went on strike (during a time when [[Labor union|union]]s were considered dangerous and illegal), they were fired.  

In [[1898]], after witnessing a series of similar strikes (many ending in police confrontation), Czolgosz returned home, where he was constantly at odds with his family's [[Catholicism|Roman Catholic]] beliefs and with his stepmother. He became a recluse, and spent much of his time alone, reading [[Socialism|socialist]] and [[Anarchism|anarchist]] newspapers. Some speculate that Czolgosz may have suffered a [[nervous breakdown|mental breakdown]]. He was very affected by hearing a speech of [[Emma Goldman]], and sought her out in New York City to discuss political matters.  She later wrote a piece sympathetic to Czolgosz's assassination of McKinley, though not quite in favor of the act. However, Czolgosz, as far as is known, failed to be accepted into any anarchist group. Indeed, his fanaticism and comments about violence aroused their suspicions; some even thought he may have been a covert government agent.

The [[radical]] ''Free Society'' [[newspaper]] issued a warning pertaining to Czolgosz reading:

:&quot;The attention of the comrades is called to another [[spy]]. He is well dressed, of medium height, rather narrow shouldered, blond, and about 25 years of age. Up to the present he has made his appearance in [[Chicago, Illinois|Chicago]] and [[Cleveland]]. In the former place he remained a short time, while in Cleveland he disappeared when the comrades had confirmed themselves of his identity and were on the point interested in the cause, asking for names, or soliciting aid for acts of contemplated [[violence]]. If this individual makes his appearance elsewhere, the comrades are warned in advance and can act accordingly.&quot;


Czolgosz's experiences had convinced him there was a great injustice in American society, an inequity which allowed the wealthy to enrich themselves by exploiting the poor. He concluded the reason for this was the structure of government itself. Then on [[July 29]], [[1900]], [[Umberto I of Italy|King Umberto I]] was assassinated by avowed anarchist, [[Gaetano Bresci]]. Bresci told the press he had to take matters into his own hands for the sake of the common man. The assassination sent shockwaves through the American anarchist movement. In Bresci, Czolgosz found his hero: a man who had the courage to sacrifice himself for the cause. When he was later arrested, police found a folded newspaper clipping about Bresci in Czolgosz’s pocket.

On [[August 31]], [[1901]] he moved to [[Buffalo, New York|Buffalo]], [[New York]] and rented a room near the site of the [[Pan-American Exposition]]. On [[September 6]], Czolgosz went to the exposition with a [[pistol]] in his hand, concealed in a handkerchief. McKinley had been standing in a receiving line greeting the public for several minutes when Czolgosz reached the front of the line and shot McKinley twice at point-blank range.  McKinley would die from his wounds on [[September 14]].

The gun used by Czolgosz was a .32 caliber Iver-Johnson &quot;Safety Automatic&quot; [[revolver]], serial number 463344. Czolgosz purchased the gun on [[September 2]], [[1901]].

After a short trial, Czolgosz was found guilty and executed by [[electric chair|electrocution]], by three jolts at 1700 volts each, on [[October 29]], [[1901]], in Auburn prison in [[Auburn, New York]]. His last words were &quot;I killed the President because he was the enemy of the good people&amp;mdash;the good working people. I am not sorry for my crime.&quot;  
As the prison guards strapped him into the chair, however, he did say through clenched teeth, &quot;I am sorry I could not see my father.&quot;  [[Emma Goldman]] was arrested on suspicion of being involved in the assassination, but was released because there was no evidence to support this suspicion.

Czolgosz's story, along with those of 8 other presidential assassins and would-be assassins, was the basis of [[Stephen Sondheim|Sondheim]]'s and [[John Weidman|Weidman's]] Broadway musical ''[[Assassins (musical)|Assassins]].'' In the musical, he is befriended by fellow assassins [[Charles J. Guiteau|Charles Guiteau]] and [[John Wilkes Booth]], also the only other two characters with &quot;ballads&quot;. His song, '''The Ballad of Czolgosz''', is an upbeat, folk song that contrasts sharply with his friend Booth's earlier ballad.

See also: [[List of assassins]]

==External References==
* [http://memory.loc.gov/mbrs/lcmp001/m1b38298.rm Film]: Reenactment of the execution of [[Leon Czolgosz]] in the electric chair, early film from [[1901]], Library of Congress archives (.rm format; offline viewable)

* [http://www.pbs.org/wgbh/amex/1900/peopleevents/pande16.html PBS biography of Czolgosz]


