      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Film |
| name = Joe Versus the Volcano
| image = Joe_Versus_The_Volcano.jpg
| caption = Joe Versus The Volcano Poster
| director = [[John Patrick Shanley]]
| writer = [[John Patrick Shanley]]
| starring = [[Tom Hanks]]&lt;br&gt;[[Meg Ryan]]
| producer = [[Steven Spielberg]]&lt;br&gt;[[Frank Marshall]]&lt;br&gt;[[Kathleen Kennedy (movie producer)|Kathleen Kennedy]]
| distributor = 
| released = [[1990]]
| runtime = 1 hr. 42 min.
| language = English
| budget = 
| imdb_id = 0099892
}}

'''''Joe Versus the Volcano''''' was a [[1990]] [[comedy]] [[film]] starring [[Tom Hanks]] and [[Meg Ryan]] and was the first film directed by screenwriter [[John Patrick Shanley]]. It was the first of many films starring Hanks and Ryan. Though it was praised by notable reviewers such as [[Roger Ebert]] at the time of its release in [[1990]], ''Joe Versus the Volcano'' was considered a box office flop, and one of [[Tom Hanks]]' minor [[film]]s. Since then, it has accumulated a cult following.

==Plot==

{{MSG:spoiler}}

Joe Banks ([[Tom Hanks]]) is a downtrodden [[everyman]], working in an factory for a [[pointy haired boss]], Frank Waturi ([[Dan Hedaya]]). Banks is chronically ill, and finds no joy in his existence.  One day, he visits a doctor ([[Robert Stack]]). The doctor diagnoses Joe with a '''brain cloud,''' cloud of black fog tissue that is located in the center of the cerebrum. It will kill him, but it has not been causing his feelings of poor health. The doctor suggests, &quot;you have some time left to you, Mr. Banks, live it well.&quot;  Joe returns to work, promptly quits his job, tells his boss off, and asks a former co-worker ([[Meg Ryan]]) out on a date. The date goes well, but after she finds out he is dying, she leaves.

The next day, Joe is visited in his apartment by a wealthy industrialist ([[Lloyd Bridges]]) who has a proposition for him. There is a small [[Pacific]] island inhabited by a tribe. They have a mineral that the industrialist needs, but they won't sell it to him. The tribe believes that the volcano on their island must be appeased with a voluntary human sacrifice once every century. None of the tribesmen is willing to sacrifice himself. If the industrialist can provide them with a willing sacrifice, they will give him the mineral. The industrialist, having heard of Joe's situation, offers Joe the opportunity to &quot;live like a king, die like a man.&quot;  He will give Joe that chance, if he will throw himself into the volcano. Joe agrees.

Joe spends a day and night on the town in New York, where he receives stylistic and self-analytical assistance from a wise chauffeur ([[Ossie Davis]]), and purchases many expensive items, including steamer trunks and a tuxedo. He flies to Los Angeles, where he is met by one of the industrialist's daughters, Angelica (also [[Meg Ryan]]). Finally, he is brought to the yacht, captained by the industrialist's other daughter Patricia (also, also [[Meg Ryan]]).

The ocean voyage starts out well enough and an emotional connection begins to form between Joe and Patricia. All is interrupted when a typhoon strikes and the yacht is sunk.  Joe rescues Patricia and fashions a raft out of his steamer trunks. Patricia has been hurt and does not regain consciousness for many days. Joe rations out the small amount of fresh water he had with him to her. When Patricia awakens, they find they have arrived at their destination, Waponi Wu.

The Waponis treat them to a grand feast before the ceremony. The chief asks one last time if anyone else will volunteer, but none will. Joe heads for the Volcano to the adoration of the tribes people. Patricia tries to stop him, declaring her love for him. He admits he loves her as well, &quot;but the timing stinks.&quot;

Patricia gets the Chief to marry them. Patricia decides to join Joe in his jump. They jump in, only to be blown out into to the ocean when the volcano erupts. The island sinks, but Joe and Patricia find a luggage raft and are saved. Upon discussion, Patricia realizes that the doctor who gave Joe a terminal diagnosis was a pawn of her father's and was lying to him. Joe is not dying and they can live [[happily ever after]].

==Trivia==
*There is a running gag throughout the film where Joe remarks to each of them that when he first saw them, he felt he had seen them somewhere before. 
*A recurring image of a jagged thunderbolt plays a part in the movie. It is part of the company logo, it is seen in the plaster damage in Joe's apartment, in the lightning bolt that destroys Patricia's boat, and in the path to the volcano. Other recurring images include ducks, the façade of the factory, dogs, and the moon. 
*[[Carol Kane]], [[Amanda Plummer]], [[Abe Vigoda]] and [[Nathan Lane]] make cameos in this film.
*The Waponi tribe is a bizarre cultural mix of [[Polynesian]], [[Hebrews|Hebrew]], [[Latin]], and [[Celt|Celtic]] influences.
*According to people close to Shanley at the time, the story is based on a near death experience Shanley had and is his attempt to describe and explain the altered outlook on life he got as a result.
*The original screenplay had a somewhat different ending with the doctor and the industrialist getting their comeuppance.
*Shanley wrote two songs for the movie, 'Marooned Without You' and 'The Cowboy Song'; the latter is performed by Tom Hanks.

==External link==
* {{imdb title|id=0099892|title=Joe versus the Volcano}}

[[Category:1990 films]]

[[de:Joe gegen den Vulkan]]</text>
